<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert of Bellême</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1052–after 1130</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Robert of Bellême, 3rd Earl of Shrewsbury, Robert de Bellême</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert of Bellême was an Anglo-Norman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-de-montgomery">Roger de Montgomery</a>, with great estates in Normandy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. He became Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a> in 1098 and was notorious for his cruelty. His rebellion against Henry I in 1102 failed when his Welsh allies, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iorwerth-ap-bleddyn">Iorwerth ap Bleddyn</a>, changed sides, and he was banished. Captured in 1112, he spent the rest of his life in prison.</p></div>
